  "content": {
    "id": "p14-23d-q5-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "5",
    "prompt": "Find all integer values of $x$ for which $\\dfrac{x^3+2x^2+1}{x-2}$ is not in lowest terms.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$x=2+17k$, where $k\\in\\mathbb Z$ and $k\\ne0$.\n\nDivision gives remainder $17$, so the numerator and denominator share a factor precisely when $x-2$ is a nonzero multiple of $17$.",
      "worked": "Polynomial long division gives quotient $x^2+4x+8$ and remainder $17$:\n$$\\begin{align*}x^3+2x^2+1&=(x-2)x^2+(4x^2+1)\\\\x^3+2x^2+1&=(x-2)(x^2+4x)+(8x+1)\\\\x^3+2x^2+1&=(x-2)(x^2+4x+8)+17.\\end{align*}$$\nFor integer $x\\ne2$, any common divisor of the numerator and denominator must divide $17$, and conversely any common divisor of $x-2$ and $17$ divides the numerator. Since $17$ is prime, the fraction is not in lowest terms exactly when $17$ divides $x-2$.\n$$x=2+17k,\\qquad k\\in\\mathbb Z,\\quad k\\ne0.$$\nThe value $x=2$ is excluded because the fraction is undefined.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 60
  },
